VALUES
  (
    'bus-networks-26-cities-open-timetables',
    'I measured the bus networks of 26 cities from their open timetables. Here is what a smarter network could save.',
    'An open tool, a lot of open data, and an honest list of what it cannot tell you.',
    $body_bus_networks_26_cities_open_timetables$<p>I live in Baku. Like most people here I take buses, and like most people I had opinions about them: routes that wander, buses that run side by side along the same avenue, stops so close together that the bus barely gets going before it brakes again. I wanted to know whether those opinions held up, and by how much the network could do better with the buses it already has. The data to check that properly isn&#39;t public. So I built what I could from what is.</p>
<h2>What the tool does</h2>
<p>It reads a city&#39;s GTFS feed, the open timetable format that most transit agencies publish for journey planners. From it, the tool measures the network the way planners do: how far each route strays from a straight line, how many routes repeat each other, how close the stops are, who lives more than 400 metres from a stop. Then it builds a travel model. People live where Kontur&#39;s population grid says they do, and travel to the places OpenStreetMap maps: schools, hospitals, shops, offices, stations. Every trip is routed door to door, with the walk to the stop, the wait, the ride, the changes.</p>
<p>On that model, the tool searches for a better network one step at a time: merging routes that repeat each other, dropping routes that carry few people, straightening detours, moving buses from quiet routes to busy ones. It keeps a change only if the average door-to-door trip gets shorter, and only if it leaves no more than a few hundred people without a stop within walking distance (500 per change, 3,000 in all). Then it checks the result on a different sample of trips than the one it searched on, and works out what the new network is worth: minutes per trip, buses, bus-hours, diesel and CO₂.</p>
<p>It is a few thousand lines of Python with no dependencies beyond the standard library, and everything is open: <a href="https://github.com/ismatsamadov/route_efficiency">https://github.com/ismatsamadov/route_efficiency</a></p>
<h2>Baku first</h2>
<p>For Baku the tool reads the routes and stops on AYNA&#39;s public map, and BakuBus&#39;s published passenger counts keep the model honest. With the same 2,015 buses, the redesign makes the average trip about three quarters of a minute shorter. Kept to today&#39;s trip times instead, it needs 219 fewer buses, which would save about 26,000 litres of diesel and 71 tonnes of CO₂ every weekday.</p>
<p>That is less than I expected, and that turned out to be the interesting part.</p>
<h2>Then 25 more cities</h2>
<p>The same tool runs on any city with an open feed. I took 25 more, on five continents, choosing feeds that their operators or cities publish under licences that allow redistribution: from Riga, Dublin, Cardiff, Sofia, Kyiv and Valencia to Halifax, Québec City, Austin, Kuala Lumpur, Sendai, Christchurch, Addis Ababa and Accra. Every city got the same settings.</p>
<p>With the same buses, the average trip gets shorter in every one of them: by 2.8 minutes in the middle city, from under a minute in Kyiv to more than eight in Trujillo. Kept to today&#39;s trip times, the 23 networks published by their operators would need about a fifth fewer buses, saving some 140,000 litres of fuel and 370 tonnes of CO₂ a weekday between them.</p>
<p>Baku, read from its own feed with the same settings as everyone else, lands near the bottom: the search finds less to improve there than in most cities.</p>
<h2>What it cannot tell you</h2>
<p>Here is the part that matters most. Outside Baku, no city in the study shares passenger counts in a form the model can use, so demand is modelled, not measured. A route the model thinks is empty may be full of students at 8 a.m. The model also sees buses only: in cities where trams or a metro carry much of the load, it underrates the network. Three of the networks come from OpenStreetMap mapping projects rather than from their operators&#39; timetables, and their numbers are the roughest. Every change the tool proposes is a candidate for a planner to check, not a plan.</p>
<p>What would make the numbers real is simple to say: an operator&#39;s counts by route. The method and the code are the same everywhere, so a city that shares its counts gets the treatment Baku has, and I would gladly run it.</p>
<h2>Try it</h2>
<p>The write-up, with the table for every city and the list of cities I left out (and why): <a href="https://github.com/ismatsamadov/route_efficiency/blob/8f75662f8c456910cd8708df1f13ec6406ec32f1/docs/world.md">https://github.com/ismatsamadov/route_efficiency/blob/8f75662f8c456910cd8708df1f13ec6406ec32f1/docs/world.md</a></p>
<p>The data, as the results were made from it: <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/ismatsamadov/route-efficiency-cities">https://huggingface.co/datasets/ismatsamadov/route-efficiency-cities</a></p>
<p>Baku&#39;s results, for readers who will not open the code: <a href="https://ismatsamadov.github.io/route_efficiency/">https://ismatsamadov.github.io/route_efficiency/</a></p>
<p>If you plan bus networks, or work at one of these agencies, I would love to hear where the model is wrong.</p>
<hr>
<p><em>Also published on <a href="https://ismatsamadov.medium.com/i-measured-the-bus-networks-of-26-cities-from-their-open-timetables-aae0aabf710a">Medium</a>.</em></p>
